        "explanation": "Regression to the mean is a statistical phenomenon where extreme initial measurements tend to be closer to the average on remeasurement, purely due to natural variability and measurement error, not any true biological change. Lead time bias relates specifically to screening and survival time interpretation, not repeated measurement of the same variable. The Hawthorne effect refers to behavior change due to awareness of being observed, not the specific statistical remeasurement phenomenon here. The placebo effect involves a perceived benefit from an inert treatment, not applicable since no treatment was given. Confounding involves a third variable distorting an exposure-outcome relationship, unrelated to this natural regression phenomenon.",
