        "explanation": "This scenario illustrates that with very large sample sizes, even a tiny, clinically trivial difference (1 mmHg) can become statistically significant, demonstrating that statistical significance does not automatically equate to clinical importance or practical relevance. The first option is the exact misconception this example is meant to correct, so it's incorrect. A very low p-value does not, by itself, guarantee clinical meaningfulness, since it is heavily influenced by sample size, contrary to that option. Larger sample sizes actually increase the likelihood of detecting even small differences as statistically significant, not reduce it. The result does not prove 'no real effect'; it shows a statistically real but clinically negligible average effect.",
