        "topic": "Environmental Health",
        "subtopic": "Environmental Sanitation Definition",
        "source": "Qbank",
        "difficulty": "Easy",
        "question": "According to WHO, 'environmental sanitation' broadly encompasses control of which of the following factors in man's physical environment that could exercise a deleterious effect on health?",
        "options": [
            "Only excreta and sewage disposal, with no other components",
            "Only vaccination programs",
            "Only occupational safety measures within factories",
            "Only air pollution control",
            "Water supply, sewage disposal, vector control, and food hygiene"
        ],
        "correct": 4,
        "explanation": "WHO's broad definition of environmental sanitation encompasses control of a wide range of environmental factors including water supply, excreta and sewage disposal, refuse/solid waste disposal, vector control, food hygiene, housing conditions, and other environmental factors in man's physical environment that could adversely affect physical development, health, or survival. It is not narrowly limited to excreta and sewage disposal alone; this represents just one component of the much broader environmental sanitation concept. It is not limited to air pollution control alone; this too is just one component within the comprehensive environmental sanitation framework, not its entire scope. It does not refer specifically or exclusively to occupational safety within factories, which would be a more specific subset falling under occupational health rather than the broader environmental sanitation concept. Vaccination programs fall under immunization/preventive medicine services, an entirely separate public health domain from environmental sanitation, which specifically concerns physical environmental conditions.",
        "id": "COMM-0316"
