        "explanation": "Modern WHO-aligned guidance recommends at least two sputum samples (typically a spot sample and an early morning sample) for smear microscopy, which balances diagnostic yield with feasibility, though many national programs historically used a three-sample approach (spot-morning-spot) for smear microscopy-based diagnosis. A single sample alone provides lower diagnostic sensitivity compared to using two (or the traditional three) samples, reducing case detection accuracy. Ten samples over ten days would be excessive, impractical, and not aligned with any standard national or WHO TB diagnostic protocol. There is no specific requirement that samples must be collected only during nighttime; standard protocols typically emphasize an early morning sample (when bacterial concentration tends to be higher after overnight pooling of secretions) combined with a spot sample, not exclusively nighttime collection. Sputum examination remains a core diagnostic method for pulmonary TB; blood tests alone (without sputum/molecular testing) are not the standard recommended diagnostic approach for confirming pulmonary TB.",
