        "explanation": "Without intervention, mother-to-child HIV transmission risk (combining pregnancy, delivery, and breastfeeding transmission routes) is estimated at approximately 15-45%, but comprehensive PMTCT interventions (maternal antiretroviral therapy, safe delivery practices, infant prophylaxis, and appropriate infant feeding counseling) can reduce this risk to less than 5%, representing one of public health's major HIV prevention success stories. The baseline risk without intervention is clearly substantial (not near 0%), which is precisely why PMTCT interventions were developed and are so impactful. The baseline risk, while significant, is not 100% (transmission is not universal even without any intervention), and effective interventions CAN and DO substantially reduce this risk, contrary to any claim of no possible reduction. Mother-to-child transmission occurs through specific biological exposure routes (in utero, intrapartum, breastfeeding) entirely different from casual household contact, which carries essentially no HIV transmission risk at all. PMTCT interventions are extensively evidence-based to REDUCE, not increase, transmission risk, representing one of the most successful HIV prevention strategies globally.",
