        "explanation": "HIV post-exposure prophylaxis should be initiated as soon as possible after a significant exposure, ideally within 72 hours (with earlier initiation, such as within hours, providing even greater effectiveness), since PEP's effectiveness in preventing infection decreases significantly with delayed initiation. Waiting up to 30 days without urgency would severely undermine PEP's effectiveness, since the intervention specifically depends on prompt initiation to interrupt early viral establishment. While baseline HIV testing of the exposed person is indeed part of proper PEP protocol (to establish that they were not already HIV-positive before exposure), this baseline testing should be done promptly alongside PEP initiation, not by waiting weeks to confirm status before considering starting the time-sensitive prophylaxis. PEP effectiveness is clearly and significantly time-sensitive, with the window for meaningful benefit closing progressively after exposure, directly contradicting any claim of no time-sensitivity. Starting PEP only after more than a week following exposure would fall well outside the effective window for benefit, making this an incorrect and clinically inappropriate recommendation.",
