        "explanation": "Mandatory screening of donated blood for major bloodborne pathogens (HIV, Hepatitis B, Hepatitis C, syphilis) is a critical blood safety measure specifically designed to prevent transfusion-transmitted infections, protecting the health of blood recipients who would otherwise be at risk of acquiring these serious infections through contaminated blood products. Screening has no relationship to blood's taste or appearance, which are irrelevant considerations for a bodily fluid used medically, not consumed; this option misunderstands the purpose entirely. Screening does not increase blood volume collected; it is a quality/safety testing process applied to already-collected donations, unrelated to donation volume itself. Comprehensive infectious disease screening actually adds to, rather than reduces, the cost and complexity of blood collection and processing, representing a necessary safety investment rather than a cost-reduction measure. Laboratory screening complements, rather than replaces, donor history screening (which includes risk behavior questionnaires); both approaches work together as part of a comprehensive blood safety strategy, not as substitutes for one another.",
