        "correct": 0,
        "explanation": "Hepatitis A virus is primarily transmitted through the fecal-oral route, typically via contaminated food and water, or through direct person-to-person contact in conditions of poor sanitation and hygiene, making it closely linked to water/sanitation infrastructure quality. Unlike Hepatitis B and C, Hepatitis A is NOT primarily transmitted through blood transfusion; blood-borne transmission is not its predominant or classically significant route, unlike the fecal-oral pathway. While rare sexual transmission (particularly through certain sexual practices involving fecal-oral exposure) can occur, this is not the PRIMARY transmission route for Hepatitis A, unlike Hepatitis B which has well-established significant sexual transmission. Mother-to-child transmission is not a significant or primary transmission route for Hepatitis A, unlike Hepatitis B, which has well-documented perinatal transmission risk. Hepatitis A has no vector-borne transmission component; no insect vector is involved in its transmission, which occurs through direct fecal-oral contamination pathways.",
