        "explanation": "Hepatitis B virus is transmitted through parenteral (blood/blood product) exposure, sexual contact, and perinatal transmission from an infected mother to her infant, distinctly different from Hepatitis A's fecal-oral transmission pattern, reflecting the virus's presence in blood and other body fluids rather than fecal shedding as the primary transmission mechanism. It is specifically NOT primarily transmitted via the fecal-oral route like Hepatitis A; this is precisely the key epidemiological distinction between these two different hepatitis viruses despite both causing liver inflammation. Hepatitis B is not an airborne infection; it requires direct exposure to infected blood or specific body fluids, not respiratory droplet transmission. There is no established significant mosquito-borne transmission route for Hepatitis B; it is not classified as a vector-borne disease. Unlike Hepatitis A (which is water/food-borne via fecal contamination), Hepatitis B transmission is not primarily linked to contaminated water sources, reflecting the fundamentally different transmission mechanisms between these hepatitis virus types.",
