        "topic": "Communicable Diseases",
        "subtopic": "Measles",
        "source": "Qbank",
        "difficulty": "Normal",
        "question": "Measles is characterized by a highly contagious transmission pattern, with a basic reproduction number (R0) typically cited in the range of?",
        "options": [
            "Less than 1",
            "12-18",
            "5-7",
            "1-2",
            "2-3"
        ],
        "correct": 1,
        "explanation": "Measles has one of the highest basic reproduction numbers among known infectious diseases, typically cited in the range of 12-18, reflecting its extremely high contagiousness through airborne transmission, and explaining why very high vaccination coverage (typically above 95%) is required to achieve herd immunity and interrupt transmission. An R0 of 1-2 would represent a much less contagious disease, far below measles' actual well-documented high transmissibility. An R0 of 2-3 is more typical of diseases like influenza or the original SARS-CoV-2 strain, not measles, which is notably more contagious than these examples. An R0 of 5-7, while higher, still substantially underestimates measles' actual documented high transmissibility compared to the more accurately cited 12-18 range. An R0 of less than 1 would mean the disease is naturally dying out/not sustaining transmission, which drastically contradicts measles' well-known status as one of the most contagious human diseases known.",
