        "explanation": "Persistent polio transmission in remaining endemic areas like parts of Pakistan is attributed to a combination of challenges including vaccine hesitancy/refusal in certain communities (sometimes linked to misinformation or mistrust), security concerns affecting vaccination team access in certain areas, and cross-border population movement facilitating continued virus transmission across geographic boundaries, all complicating full eradication efforts. Vaccine availability itself is generally not the primary limiting challenge in these contexts; the barriers are more related to access, acceptance, and security issues rather than simple supply shortage. The very premise of ongoing polio eradication challenges directly contradicts any claim of 'complete absence of poliovirus circulation,' since ongoing wild poliovirus transmission is precisely why Pakistan remains classified as endemic, necessitating continued intensive eradication efforts. The described challenges (hesitancy, insecurity, cross-border movement) directly demonstrate that universal, unanimous community acceptance does NOT exist, contrary to that option's claim. The premise of the question itself explicitly states that wild poliovirus transmission has NOT been fully interrupted in Pakistan, directly contradicting any claim that polio has already been completely eradicated there with no remaining cases.",
        "id": "COMM-0374"
