        "explanation": "Depression is recognized by WHO and Global Burden of Disease studies as one of the leading causes of years lived with disability (YLD) globally, representing a substantial component of the overall global disease burden and highlighting mental health's significant, often underrecognized public health importance alongside physical health conditions. This directly contradicts any claim of 'no significant disability or disease burden,' since depression is specifically and prominently highlighted as a MAJOR contributor to global disability burden in these authoritative estimates. Describing depression's impact as merely 'mild, insignificant' with no need for public health attention profoundly understates its substantial documented burden and directly contradicts the WHO's own emphasis on mental health as a priority global health issue. Depression affects individuals across ALL age groups, including children, adolescents, working-age adults, and the elderly, not exclusively elderly populations; this is an important consideration for comprehensive public health mental health strategies spanning the entire lifespan. Depression is NOT completely treatment-resistant; effective treatments including psychotherapy and pharmacotherapy exist and can significantly improve outcomes for many affected individuals, making this an inaccurate and unnecessarily pessimistic characterization of the condition's treatability.",
