        "explanation": "Evidence-based road safety interventions including helmet laws for motorcyclists/cyclists, seatbelt use laws, speed limit enforcement, and drink-driving laws have all been demonstrated to significantly reduce road traffic injury and fatality rates, forming core components of the WHO's recommended road safety strategy framework (the 'Save LIVES' approach and similar frameworks). Removing traffic laws entirely would be expected to dramatically INCREASE, not decrease, road traffic injuries and fatalities, directly contradicting the evidence-based approach to road safety improvement. Eliminating speed limits would similarly be expected to increase crash severity and fatality risk, given the well-established relationship between vehicle speed and both crash likelihood and injury severity; this contradicts established road safety evidence. Road signage plays an important role in guiding safe driver behavior and hazard awareness; removing it entirely would likely increase confusion and crash risk, not improve road safety. Public transportation, when properly regulated and maintained, is generally associated with LOWER per-passenger-mile injury/fatality rates compared to private vehicle travel in many contexts; banning it entirely would not align with evidence-based road safety improvement strategies and could paradoxically increase overall road risk by shifting more travel to private vehicles.",
